# hi's own flags, one row each:
#   <flag>|<argument>|<needs>|<script var>|<first arg>|<help>
#
# The one place they are spelled. hi.sh reads it to dispatch (a row with a
# script var execs ${!var} with the arg first; the rest are hi.sh's own case
# arms) and to print --help; common/targets.sh reads it so `hi --<TAB>` offers
# the same list. <argument> is what the flag takes on the command line, as
# --help shows it (empty for a bare flag); the shape is part of the 1.x
# contract, so it lives here rather than in prose. <help> is one clause -
# docs/hi.1 is the long form, drift-checked against this by
# tests/hi/parse_test.sh. <needs> is what a flag wants that the payload does
# not carry: `-` for nothing (works in a session), `scripts` for scripts/ (a
# package ships it, a session does not), `git` for .git (neither a package
# nor a session has it). Rows print in this order.
--help||-|||this text
--version||-|||hi's version: the packaging stamp, or git describe
--use|<backend>|-|||skip probing and force that backend (ssh counts as one)
--plain||-|||a bare shell, nothing copied: no tar or /tmp needed
--mux||-|||the session inside a local tmux, one named session per target; a repeat reattaches
--preview-packages||-|||the package-priority legend, as the header prints it
--doctor|[--json] [--use <backend>] [target]|scripts|_HI_DOCTOR||a read-only pre-flight report; --json for a bug report
--install|[--yes] [--no-link] [--prefix <dir>]|scripts|_HI_INSTALL||install or repair say-hi's lines in your shell rc files
--uninstall||scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--uninstall|take those lines back out again
--configure|[--preset <name>]|scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--features-only|revisit the settings, leaving the rc wiring be
--overlay-init||scripts|_HI_INSTALL|--overlay-init|seed the config overlay with defaults, git it in place
--preview-colors||scripts|_HI_COLOR_PREVIEW||every ssh host and your user, in their resolved colors
--update|[<ref>] [git-pull-options]|git|||git pull in the say-hi checkout, or check out a release tag or branch (needs its .git)
